<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F6.13" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S5.F6.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:113%;">DMA prototypes of grounded modes.<span id="S5.F6.14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We cluster <span id="S5.F6.14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">astronaut</span> by ethnicity, <span id="S5.F6.14.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dog</span> by breed, and <span id="S5.F6.14.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">car</span> by model or color.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LoRA and Textual Inversion.</span>
Figure <a href="#S5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 5.3 Mode Averages ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Diffusion Mental Averages" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows that using LoRA or Textual Inversion helps guide the model toward cluster-specific regions, producing averages that better reflect the modes’ semantics, with LoRA better preserving color and car shape than Textual Inversion.
This suggests that both methods help capture semantics beyond the <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-space, including aspects typically encoded in the noisy latent, such as color and image layout <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">95</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">100</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
