<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5 </span>Generalization Across Architectures</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We extend DMA to DiT-XL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite>, a class-conditional, transformer-based diffusion model. Since DiT lacks a U-Net-style semantic bottleneck like the <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-space, we instead use the output of the final transformer block, which yields the most consistent results (Figure <a href="#S5.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 5.5 Generalization Across Architectures ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Diffusion Mental Averages" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). See Appendix <a href="#S1.SS4" title="A.4 DiT Implementation Details ‣ A Additional Implementation Details ‣ Diffusion Mental Averages" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a> and <a href="#S3.SS7" title="C.7 DiT Semantic Layer Selection ‣ C Ablation Studies ‣ Diffusion Mental Averages" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.7</span></a> for implementation details and block comparisons.</p>
</div>
