<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F16.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Figure 16</span>: </span><span id="S3.F16.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:113%;">Effects of CFG scale on visual quality.<span id="S3.F16.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Low CFG scales fail to generate faithful structures (as seen in <span id="S3.F16.10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">monstera</span>), while excessively high CFG scales lead to oversaturated colors and unnatural contrast.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.6 </span>Effects of Number of Noisy Latents (<math id="S3.SS6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze how the number of initial noisy latents, <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, affects the variability of the resulting averages. For each value of <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample four disjoint sets of <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> noisy latents, and compute an average image for each set under the same concept prompt. As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F17" title="Figure 17 ‣ C.6 Effects of Number of Noisy Latents (𝐾) ‣ C Ablation Studies ‣ Diffusion Mental Averages" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>, the variation among the four averages decreases as <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> increases, consistent with expected statistical behavior.
We find that <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=1{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=1{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficient for most concepts to appear visually consistent, whereas smaller <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> often yields noticeable differences across sets.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F17" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29239v1/FigB6.png" id="S3.F17.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:651/411;" width="651" height="411" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F17.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 17</span>: </span><span id="S3.F17.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="S3.F17.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of the number of noisy latents <math id="S3.F17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
In the blue box, we sample four distinct sets of <math id="S3.F17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=100</annotation></semantics></math> noisy latents and compute their averages. For each set, we show eight example averages (in a 4x2 grid), each computed from a random latent in that set, which converge to similar-looking images <em id="S3.F17.7.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">within each set</em>. However, at <math id="S3.F17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=100</annotation></semantics></math>, noticeable variations are still observed <em id="S3.F17.7.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">across the four sets</em> shown in the images with colored borders. As <math id="S3.F17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> increases, averages across distinct sets become more similar, consistent with the statistical behavior of larger sample sizes.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F18" class="ltx_figure">
<table id="S3.F18.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.F18.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.F18.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r" style="padding:0.85pt 1.0pt;"></td>
<th id="S3.F18.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column" style="padding:0.85pt 1.0pt;">
<span id="S3.F18.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block">
<span id="S3.F18.3.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F18.3.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Transformer</span></span>
<span id="S3.F18.3.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F18.3.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"></span><span id="S3.F18.3.1.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Block 0</span></span>
</span></th>
<th id="S3.F18.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column" style="padding:0.85pt 1.0pt;">
<span id="S3.F18.3.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block">
<span id="S3.F18.3.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F18.3.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Transformer</span></span>
